Port directions after the sbox-compatible split (2026-10-26)
===========================================================

Before: -p and --expose-port were synonyms, both meaning "the guest may reach this
host port". sbox has always meant two opposite things:
  --allow-port, -p PORT   forward a host port INTO the sandbox   (guest -> host)
  --expose-port PORT      expose a sandbox port to the host       (host -> guest)
mbox now matches sbox. --expose-port publishes the guest port on host loopback at
the same number.

Host-side assembly (wrapper --dry-run, no VM):

  $ mbox --dry-run /tmp --expose-port 8080 -p 8012 | grep -o -- '--net-default.*--port [^ ]*'
  --net-default-egress deny --net-default-ingress allow --net-rule allow:egress@host:tcp:8012 --port 127.0.0.1:8080:8080

Why blocked mode changes shape when something is published: --no-net is sugar for
--net-default deny in BOTH directions, so it would close the ingress side and
silently disable every published port. The two explicit defaults keep egress denied
while leaving ingress open for the relay.

  $ mbox --dry-run /tmp -p 8012 | grep -c -- --no-net
  1
  $ mbox --dry-run /tmp --expose-port 8080 | grep -c -- --no-net
  0

Rejection of ports outside 1..65535 (both directions), and of a missing value:

  mbox: invalid TCP port '0' (expected 1..65535)
  mbox: invalid TCP port '70000' (expected 1..65535)
  mbox: --expose-port requires a value (see mbox --help)

Real VM, both directions at once, blocked egress. Host 18011 is a python http.server
(0.0.0.0:18011); the guest listens on 18099 with busybox httpd:

  $ mbox /tmp -p 18011 -- bash -lc 'GW=$(ip route | awk "/default/{print \$3; exit}");
      echo "GW=$GW";
      busybox wget -T 8 -q -O - "http://$GW:18011/" && echo HOST-REACHABLE || echo HOST-BLOCKED;
      busybox wget -T 5 -q -O /dev/null http://93.184.216.34/ && echo EGRESS-OPEN || echo EGRESS-BLOCKED'
  GW=172.16.0.5
  host-service-page
  HOST-REACHABLE
  EGRESS-BLOCKED

  host listener log: 127.0.0.1 - - "GET / HTTP/1.1" 200 -      # the guest's request

  $ mbox /tmp --expose-port 18099 -- bash -lc 'mkdir -p /tmp/www;
      echo guest-service-page > /tmp/www/index.html; exec busybox httpd -f -p 18099 -h /tmp/www' &
  $ curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:18099/
  guest-service-page

Policy tier (no VM) with the new assembly, including the Nix evaluation of both new
options and of the renamed one:

  $ nix build .#checks.x86_64-linux.policy      # all 18 scripts pass
  $ python3 tests/mbox-network.py
  PASS: actual mbox port assembly in both directions, blocked-mode grants, invalid
  port rejection, evaluated default ports, renamed option alias

The NixOS module option that used to be exposedTCPPorts is now allowedTCPPorts
(sbox's name for the same direction) and there is a publishedTCPPorts for the new
one. exposedTCPPorts still evaluates through mkRenamedOptionModule.
